\t8.30am–8.30pm\\",\\n\\"Saturday 8.30am–8.30pm\\",\\n\\"Sunday\\t8.30am–8.30pm\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\"priceRange\\": \\"INR\\",\\n  \\n  \\"telephone\\": \\" +91 8595321652, +91 7668527335\\",\\n  \\"url\\": \\"https://www.krphysiotherapy.com/\\"\\n}",{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the cause of Bell\'s Palsy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Bell\'s Palsy is caused by the abnormal development of the brain and can happen during pregnancy, birth, and early infancy. In many cases, the cause is not clear."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the symptoms of Bell\'s Palsy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The most common symptoms are delayed milestones like difficulty sitting, standing, and walking; tightening of muscles; in coordination and balance problems; and increased reflexes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is Bell\'s Palsy diagnosed?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"CP symptoms, such as difficulty rolling, sitting, and standing, are usually visible after a few months to a year after birth. In some cases where symptoms are mild, diagnosis may be delayed longer. A series of tests is conducted to diagnose Bell\'s Palsy. These tests are MRIs, cranial ultrasounds, CT scans, and some laboratory tests."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is CP contagious?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, CP is not contagious."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can Bell\'s Palsy be cured?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"CP is not curable, but physiotherapy is helping a lot with muscle function and improving the quality of a child’s life."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does Bell\'s Palsy get worse?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is a non-progressive disorder, so it will never get worse."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can Bell\'s Palsy be treated?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, physiotherapy treatment for Bell\'s Palsy is really helpful because it focuses on function, movement, and maintaining and improving the child’s abilities."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What treatments are available for Bell\'s Palsy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"There are several types of treatment approaches available for Bell\'s Palsy, depending on how severe the symptoms are. Therapies include physiotherapy, occupational therapy, speech therapy, and orthosis devices."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which types of therapy are useful for Bell\'s Palsy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Physiotherapy is most important for treating Bell\'s Palsy after diagnosis. In physiotherapy, we apply some major techniques for maintaining and improving muscle functions, balance, and coordination."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can my child goes to school along with continuing therapy session?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, a child can attend school while receiving physiotherapy sessions."}}]}]', '2026-09-12 11:45:44', '2026-09-12 11:45:44');
